'''Alpujarra''' ({{IPA|bl|alpuˈxara}}, {{langx|ar|البشرات|translit=al-bašarāt or al-bušarāt|4=the fertile pastures}}), also known by it's indigenous [[Kurupirangüe language|Kurupirangüe]] name '''Katu-yva-kora''' ({{Literal translation|valley where the people sing by the water}}) is a large bowl-shaped valley consisting of broad and fertile lands surrounded by the [[Matarrañya|Matarrañya / Cañadapirén]] in Southern [[Atlantida]]. [[Salto Bay]] is completely surrounded by the valley, and it fills much of it's interior. 

The valley floor is an alluvial plain that formed in the [[W:Graben|graben]] (tectonic depression) between the [[Yawar Sypa]] and Pahá faults to the north and the [[Vitacura|Vitacura fault]] to the south. Within the valley and surrounding the bay on all sides are the urban communities of the [[Castejón Metropolis]] and several [[Araucaria|Araucarian]] communes, while the narrow southern reaches of the valley extend along the [[Guadalquivir]] to the [[W:Atlantic Ocean|Atlantic Ocean]].  

